# Paper Outline (Jupyter + JavaScript)

This notebook helps you outline and write the paper yourself. It provides prompts and checklists only — no generated prose.

Kernel: set to a JavaScript kernel (Node.js via IJavascript, or Deno). If you require zero Python on your system, use a browser-based JupyterLite with a JavaScript kernel.

Guidance
- Draft bullets first, then expand to paragraphs
- One idea per paragraph; lead with the takeaway
- Use the checklist to track progress
## Submission checklist (customize to your venue)
- [ ] Page limit and format OK
- [ ] Anonymization (if double-blind)
- [ ] Figures/Tables readable in grayscale
- [ ] Links in PDF clickable
- [ ] Artifact and code links included
- [ ] Ethics/conflicts/acknowledgements handled
## References (collection bucket)
- Add citations you plan to use, with links and a 1-line note
- Later move to your final reference manager or `.bib`
## Conclusion
- One-sentence summary of contribution
- One-sentence summary of results
- One-sentence on impact/future work
## Artifacts / Reproducibility
- Code and data availability
- Environment and versions
- How to reproduce results (commands)
- Checklist for artifact evaluation
## Limitations / Threats to Validity
- External validity: generalization limits
- Internal validity: confounds, missing controls
- Construct validity: measurement/definition issues
- Reliability: stability of results
## Discussion
- Interpret the results: what they say and what they do NOT say
- Practical implications and use cases
- Comparison to prior art (strengths/weaknesses)
## Validation / Evaluation
- Sanity checks and ablations
- Verification/replication steps
- Statistical tests or confidence
- Threats to validity mitigations
## Results
- What you measured: datasets, tasks, baselines
- How you measured: metrics, procedures
- Key tables/plots you will include
- Headline numbers (draft)
## Implementation
- Architecture diagram (sketch or describe)
- Stack choices (and why): language, runtime, libraries
- Key components/modules
- Complexity/performance considerations
## Strategy / Approach
- High-level idea: one paragraph
- Assumptions and scope
- Why this is better/different than prior work
- Risks and trade-offs
## Preliminaries / Background
- Terminology/glossary: define the minimum to read the paper
- Problem formalization (if applicable): inputs, outputs, constraints
- Related work (short): 3–6 key references and what they miss
## Introduction
Goal: Convince a non-expert why the problem matters and what you did.
- Hook: surprising stat, pain point, or gap
- Problem statement: who is affected and how
- Prior work gap: what's missing or hard
- Your approach: 1–2 sentences
- Contributions (bullets):
  - C1:
  - C2:
  - C3:
- Roadmap sentence: what the rest of the paper covers
## Abstract (≤ 200 words)
Prompt: What is the core contribution and why does it matter? What is the result and how do you support it? Write a single paragraph readers can skim.
- Problem:
- Approach:
- Key result:
- Evidence:
- Implication:
## Title & Metadata
- Title (working):
- Authors:
- Affiliation(s):
- Target venue(s):
- Keywords:
- Artifact URL(s):
## Progress checklist (tick as you write)
- [ ] Metadata filled (title, authors, venue)
- [ ] Abstract drafted (≤ 200 words)
- [ ] Introduction drafted
- [ ] Preliminaries/Background drafted
- [ ] Strategy/Approach drafted
- [ ] Implementation drafted
- [ ] Results drafted
- [ ] Validation/Evaluation drafted
- [ ] Discussion drafted
- [ ] Limitations/Threats drafted
- [ ] Artifacts/Reproducibility drafted
- [ ] Conclusion drafted
- [ ] References collected
- [ ] Submission checklist completed
